    "source": "sharegpt",
    "why": "Two generative tenants, and the HIGHEST-POWER scenario in the matrix. T1 established that MPS is bounded by power, not contention: every arm holding the 7B sat at 343 W of a 350 W board limit and MPS returned +0.0%, where round one at 249-256 W returned +24.9%. Three predictions registered before the run. (a) MPS again returns ~0%: adding a 1.5B to a card already at its ceiling cannot create headroom. (b) Sharing costs MORE than T1's +3.1% e2e p99, because the neighbour is a steady decode consumer rather than a bursty encoder. (c) The clock-residency gain that made bge-m3 7.4% FASTER will NOT appear for llm2 -- it only existed because the embedder alone left the card at 3.5% utilisation and 1,665 MHz. A 1.5B alone will already hold clocks near peak, so there is nothing for a neighbour to recover. T4 is the opposite extreme and the real test of the power hypothesis. OUTCOME: all three held. MPS -0.010% at 346.4 W (3.6 W headroom). 7B e2e p99 +33.8% vs T1's +3.1%. llm2 alone holds 1922 MHz -- HIGHER than the 7B's 1809 -- and sharing drops it to 1780, so it got slower on every measure. The unregistered finding is bigger than all three: ITL CONVERGES. The 7B:1.5B per-token ratio goes 2.29x dedicated to 1.03x shared, so the small model pays e2e p50 +189% while the big one pays +38%, on total throughput that moved -0.59%. Zero preemptions, KV 1.5-14.3%: contention, not queueing. FINDINGS 2.11."
  },
